{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, sorry about the rough morning. I've passed your desktop (BL-DT-0108) to our Tier 2 engineers, since it's showing offline and I can't fix network connectivity from here. Your account looks fine: no sign-in problems and no blocks. For the floor 2 printer (BL-PR-FL2), it's showing offline for everyone, so the printer itself is the likely problem. If someone is near it, could they check the front screen for errors like a paper jam, low paper or toner, or an open door, clear any error, then turn the printer off for 30 seconds and back on, and check that the network cable is firmly plugged in with a link light on? If it's still offline after that, reply here and we'll escalate it. Until it's back, you can print to another printer on a different floor.",
  "internal_note": "Ticket EVAL-misc-03, user James O'Connor (user_id 8, Warehouse Supervisor, Chicago, usual hours 06:00-14:00). Triage said user_blocked true, but the account is active with sign_in_blocked false, 0 failed logins, and VPN access true, so the block flag in triage is incorrect. Sign-in logs for the last 14 days show 10 successful sign-ins (corporate M365 and residential VPN, Duo push), with no failures, no MFA denials, and no unusual hours. Desktop BL-DT-0108 (Windows 11, 78% disk free, no warnings) shows online:false with last check-in 2026-10-11 01:12 UTC. No KB article covers desktop network connectivity, so I escalated it rather than troubleshooting from general knowledge. Printer BL-PR-FL2 (firmware 4.2) shows online:false with no warnings and last check-in 2026-10-10 22:12 UTC. Per KB-022 the printer is offline for everyone, so the printer is the problem. I gave the user the KB-022 on-site steps (clear error, power cycle, check cable and link light). Escalate to hardware/network if it stays offline after the power cycle. No fixes were queued and no actions were taken on any account.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-022"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
